  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1049 PM CDT SUN JUL 19 2026\n\nThe work week begins with Central Alabama firmly under the\ninfluence of a broad high-pressure ridge centered over the Great\nPlains, which is gradually building eastward. This synoptic setup\nsupports a \"Major HeatRisk\" across the South, with temperatures\nclimbing into the upper 90s and heat indices reaching triple\ndigits through the first half of the work week. Regarding heat,\nheat index values across the northwest counties (I-59 northward)\nmay approach advisory thresholds. However, for the central\ncorridor (I-59 to I-85), subsidence aloft may allow for lower\nsurface dewpoints than model guidance suggests, potentially\nlimiting the spatial coverage of the most oppressive conditions.\nSouthern counties (I-85 and south) are expected to remain below\nadvisory criteria as increasing cloud cover and convective debris\nfrom the Gulf system help to moderate temperatures.\n\nWhile several shortwaves will bring frontal boundaries toward the\nSoutheast by mid-week, the primary focus remains the tropical\ndepression currently in the northern Gulf. This system is\nexpected to intensify into a tropical storm by Monday morning.\nThe steering environment is currently characterized by weak\ncurrents, but as the high-pressure ridge over the central U.S.\nbuilds mid-week, it will become the dominant steering feature.\nThere remains uncertainty regarding the exact track: if the high\npressure is robust and moves across the MS/AL region, the system\nwill be forced westward toward Texas, keeping the most\nsignificant tropical impacts away from Central Alabama.\nConversely, a weaker high-pressure ridge could allow for a more\nnorthwesterly track closer to the region. The official forecast\ncurrently reflects a blend of these guidance solutions pending\nfurther model consensus.\n\nImpacts for Central Alabama are expected to remain minimal at\nthis time, though the period from Tuesday night through Wednesday\nrequires close monitoring. During this window, winds are forecast\nto transition to a southerly or southeasterly direction. While\nconfidence is currently insufficient to warrant a Wind Advisory,\nlargely due to model divergence regarding the system's intensity,\ngusty winds remain possible if the low maintains a track in\ncloser proximity to our area. Regarding convection, isolated to\nscattered storms on Monday will likely transition into increased\ncoverage by Tuesday night and Wednesday, particularly across our\nfar southeastern counties, where the influence of the tropical\nmoisture feed will be most pronounced. Across the broader CWA,\ninstability will remain high, maintaining the potential for\nstrong to isolated severe thunderstorms each day from Monday\nthrough Wednesday.\n\nBy Thursday, the synoptic pattern undergoes a significant shift\nas the aforementioned frontal boundary, pushed by a shortwave\ntrough tracking through the Tennessee Valley, sweeps through the\nSoutheast. This transition is expected to displace the lingering\ntropical moisture and shift the local flow to a northerly or\nnortheasterly direction. This will effectively end the persistent\ntriple-digit heat index threat, bringing slightly cooler and\ndrier air into the region. The remainder of the period, from\nFriday through Saturday, should offer some relief from the\noppressive conditions of the early week.\n\n16",
